clueweb09-enwp00-38-14723.240 carcinoma and is potentially malignant. Atypical squamous (resembling fish scales) cells proliferate through the whole thickness of the epidermis. The lesions may occur anywhere on the skin surface or on mucosal surfaces. The cause most frequently found is trivalent arsenic compounds. Freezing, cauterization or diathermy coagulation is often effective treatment. [edit] Histology Bowen's is equivalent to squamous cell carcinoma in situ. The entire tumor is confined to the epidermis and does not invade into the dermis. The cells in Bowen's are extremely unusual or atypical under the microscope and in many cases look worse under the microscope than the cells of many outright and invading squamous-cell carcinomas. The degree of atypia (strangeness, unusualness) seen under the microscope best tells how cells may behave should they invade another portion of the body. [edit] Treatment Photodynamic therapy (PDT), Cryotherapy (freezing) or local chemotherapy (with 5-fluorouracil) are favored by some clinicians over excision.
clueweb09-enwp00-38-14723.360 cells may behave should they invade another portion of the body. [edit] Treatment Photodynamic therapy (PDT), Cryotherapy (freezing) or local chemotherapy (with 5-fluorouracil) are favored by some clinicians over excision. Because the cells of Bowen's disease have not invaded the dermis, it has a much better prognosis than invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Outstanding result have been noted with the use of imiquimod for Bowen's disease of the skin, including the penis (erythroplasia of Queyrat). Imiquimod is not FDA approved for the treatment of squamous cell carcinoma. [edit] Additional images [] [] [] [edit] External links Photo Histological slide v ýýý d ýýý e Tumors: skin cancer (C43-C44/D22-D23, 172-173/216) Epidermis Melanoma (Superficial spreading melanoma, Nodular melanoma, Acral lentiginous melanoma, Lentigo maligna melanoma) Basal cell carcinoma ýý Squamous cell carcinoma (Acanthoma) Bowen's disease Dermis Dermatofibroma/dermatofibrosarcoma ýý Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans Nevus Melanocytic nevus ýý Dysplastic nevus ýý Halo nevus ýý Spitz nevus see also
